Peatlands
Soil properties for one of the hydrogeological environments described in Lundmark, A., 
2008. Monitoring transport and fate of de-icing salt in the roadside environment - Modelling 
and field measurements. Doctoral thesis in Land and Water Resources Engineering, 
TRITA-LWR PHD 1038. KTH Architecture and the Built Environment.

Conceptual description:
Peatland consists of organic soils (peat) that are often underlain by clay. These areas are 
generally located in low points in the terrain, but can also be found in areas located at 
discharge points from eskers and in depressions higher up in the terrain. The groundwater 
level is typically shallow, with a depth of about 0-1 m. Peatlands can be divided into fens 
and peat bogs. Fens are by definition discharge areas and the bogs are usually drained to
nearby ditches. The peat bogs usually consist of peat with a thickness of >2 m, whereas 
the fens are usually relatively thin, having a peat thickness of at the most 1-2 m.

Soil profile:
Peat 0-4 m depth
Clay 4-6 m depth
Till 6-7 m depth
Bedrock >7 m depth

Brooks and Corey parameters for clay and till represent effective parameters 
reported in Lundmark & Jansson: Generic soil descriptions for modelling water and chloride
dynamics in the unsaturated zone based on Swedish soils. Submitted December 2007 to
Geoderma. Parameters for peat are taken from profile 513:1 Falköping.

2008-03-17 Annika Lundmark 
